Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The prior strategy proved that explicit opening/closing of a constrained span
// fixes extraction, but the evaluation preview `SELECT <id> FROM <id>>>` shows a
// subtler issue: we were closing as soon as the parser said the prefix was
// complete. For SQL grammars, a very short query can already be complete, so the
// strategy systematically preferred prematurely minimal statements over the
// semantically correct full query. In Spider, that destroys accuracy even though
// syntax and `<< >>` extraction are perfect.
//
// This revision changes the stopping policy inside the constrained span:
//
// 1) We still explicitly open `<<` and explicitly close with CloseConstrainedSpan,
//    so extractability remains guaranteed.
//
// 2) But we no longer close immediately on the first complete SQL prefix.
//    Instead, while inside the constrained span, we continue generating under
//    parser control until one of three events happens:
//      - the parser reaches a dead end and we roll back,
//      - the remaining global step budget is too small to safely continue, or
//      - the parser is complete and we have already spent a meaningful amount of
//        constrained generation, at which point we allow closure.
//    This biases toward fuller SQL queries rather than the earliest valid one.
//
// 3) We use ConstrainedSymbol as the main primitive, but with a dynamic local
//    budget: when the current SQL is incomplete we allow up to stepTokenBudget
//    tokens; when it is complete we reduce to a single-symbol lookahead. That
//    lets the model extend a complete-but-probably-too-short query by one more
//    grammar-valid symbol if the distribution prefers doing so, while still
//    making closure available soon after.
//
// 4) Dead-end handling remains rollback-based. After rollback, if the rolled-back
//    prefix is complete and we are near budget exhaustion, we close; otherwise
//    we keep generating. This avoids trapping the model in malformed branches
//    while preserving the possibility of a longer correct query.
//
// 5) After closing the constrained span, we stop immediately. Spider wants one
//    SQL query only, and trailing text is harmful.
//
// In short: keep the explicit delimiters, but stop treating "first complete SQL"
// as "final answer". Stay inside the grammar longer, use parser-guided lookahead
// to extend short complete prefixes, and close only after some evidence that the
// query is mature or budget pressure says to finish.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: OpenConstrainedSpan creates an empty constrained segment, so
//   validity holds from parser.IsValidPrefix([]). In each ConstrainedSymbol
//   branch, the helper returns currentOut that is a valid parser prefix, and we
//   assign currentConstrainedOut := currentOut. RollbackConstrainedSpan restores
//   a valid constrained prefix by construction. CloseConstrainedSpan sets
//   insideConstrainedOut to false, making the implication vacuous.
//
// suffix: Outside constrained mode the invariant is vacuous. Opening yields an
//   empty constrained suffix, so the suffix relation holds trivially. In every
//   ConstrainedSymbol branch we compute stablePrefix by removing the old
//   constrained suffix and then set generated := stablePrefix + currentOut, so
//   generated ends with currentConstrainedOut exactly. RollbackConstrainedSpan
//   returns matching generated/current values. Closing exits constrained mode, so
//   the implication is vacuous.
//
// progress: OpenConstrainedSpan appends exactly 1 token and we increment steps by
//   1. Each ConstrainedSymbol call contributes stepsUsed generated tokens beyond
//   the prior constrained suffix, and we increment steps by the same stepsUsed;
//   thus |generated| grows by at most the increase in steps. CloseConstrainedSpan
//   appends exactly 1 token and we increment steps by 1. Rollback only shrinks or
//   preserves generated, so |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps is preserved.
//   Break branches do not change state.
//
// cost accounting: The returned cost is the local steps counter. Every helper
//   that bumps helper cost and is used here is mirrored in steps: +1 for open and
//   close, +stepsUsed for ConstrainedSymbol. Query helpers and rollback helpers
//   do not change steps. Every bumping branch checks remaining budget first, so
//   steps stays within maxSteps and cost := steps is sound.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
